= Relation — Technical =

== Extensional model ==
An n-ary relation R over domains D1...Dn is:

<pre>
R ⊆ D1 × ... × Dn
</pre>

For binary R:

<pre>
R ⊆ D × D
</pre>

Relation properties can then be defined set-theoretically.

== Common characteristics ==
Reflexive:

<pre>
∀x R(x,x)
</pre>

Symmetric:

<pre>
∀x∀y(R(x,y) → R(y,x))
</pre>

Transitive:

<pre>
∀x∀y∀z((R(x,y) ∧ R(y,z)) → R(x,z))
</pre>

Functional:

<pre>
R(x,y1) ∧ R(x,y2) → y1 = y2
</pre>

Inverse-functional properties constrain the source analogously.

== Reification / n-ary relations ==
Binary graph form can be insufficient for statements with qualifiers.

Example:

<pre>
Alice employedBy Org from 2020 to 2024
</pre>

Modeling options include:
* reified relation node;
* n-ary pattern;
* named graph / statement annotation;
* RDF 1.2 triple terms;
* event entity.

The correct choice depends on semantics and provenance needs.

== RDFS domain/range ==
RDFS domain and range are entailment rules.

If:

<pre>
p rdfs:domain C
x p y
</pre>

then RDFS entails:

<pre>
x rdf:type C
</pre>

They do not simply reject data outside C.

Constraint languages such as SHACL serve different purposes.

== OWL relation axioms ==
OWL object properties can be declared:
* symmetric;
* asymmetric;
* transitive;
* reflexive;
* irreflexive;
* functional;
* inverse-functional;
* inverse of another property.

These declarations change entailments and can interact with consistency.

